WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:06.475
In Florida’s bays and along the Intracoastal, most of the marks you’ll steer by aren’t buoys at all.

2
00:00:06.725 --> 00:00:08.475
They’re daybeacons.

3
00:00:08.725 --> 00:00:13.450
A daybeacon is a fixed mark: a signboard, called a dayboard, on a pile.

4
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:16.950
Red ones are triangles with even numbers.

5
00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.600
Keep them on your right, returning.

6
00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:22.725
Green ones are squares with odd numbers.

7
00:00:22.975 --> 00:00:25.375
Keep them on your left, returning.

8
00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:29.500
So triangles are like nuns, and squares are like cans.

9
00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:33.850
Because a daybeacon is driven into the bottom, it can’t drag.

10
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:37.225
It’s more reliable in position than a buoy.

11
00:00:37.475 --> 00:00:39.225
But here’s the catch.

12
00:00:39.475 --> 00:00:41.650
Where does a pile have to stand?

13
00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:46.725
In shallow water, right at the edge of the channel, or beyond it.

14
00:00:46.975 --> 00:00:48.875
So don’t shave it close.

15
00:00:49.125 --> 00:00:53.725
Give every daybeacon a reasonable berth, especially at low water.
